1
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:54,971
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:02,103
It's Kim Tae-ung.

3
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:05,690
My Joseon name.

4
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:08,193
Kim…

5
00:01:09,486 --> 00:01:10,820
Tae-ung.

6
00:01:13,198 --> 00:01:14,199
I like you.

7
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:27,462
I like you, Ms. Keum.

8
00:01:42,143 --> 00:01:43,144
I'm lying.

9
00:01:44,729 --> 00:01:45,563
What?

10
00:01:45,647 --> 00:01:47,398
You lied to me too

11
00:01:48,441 --> 00:01:50,110
when you said you liked me.

12
00:01:54,614 --> 00:01:56,533
But that wasn't a lie.

13
00:01:57,033 --> 00:01:59,160
I mean, that was a lie, but…

14
00:01:59,244 --> 00:02:02,122
Mr. Sato, where are you going?

15
00:02:02,205 --> 00:02:03,373
Mr. Sato.

16
00:02:03,456 --> 00:02:04,332
Mr…

17
00:02:04,874 --> 00:02:06,126
Hey, Kim Tae-ung!

18
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:17,178
Was that a dream?

19
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:31,276
Did you sleep well?

20
00:02:36,739 --> 00:02:38,032
Good morning.

21
00:02:41,119 --> 00:02:43,163
Why did I sleep here?

22
00:02:45,039 --> 00:02:46,040
Don't you remember?

23
00:02:46,624 --> 00:02:47,834
Remember what?

24
00:02:51,296 --> 00:02:53,923
I had a beer with Sato Hideo

25
00:02:54,007 --> 00:02:57,051
after work yesterday…

26
00:03:00,305 --> 00:03:01,389
Two beers, I guess?

27
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,893
I think I had about that much.

28
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:09,230
I don't remember anything after that.

29
00:03:11,316 --> 00:03:13,651
I swear. I don't remember anything at all.

30
00:03:13,735 --> 00:03:17,822
So… how did I get home yesterday?

31
00:03:25,830 --> 00:03:27,415
<i>It's Kim Tae-ung.</i>

32
00:03:28,416 --> 00:03:29,334
My Joseon name.

33
00:03:30,126 --> 00:03:30,960
Kim…

34
00:03:32,045 --> 00:03:33,004
Tae-ung.

35
00:03:40,637 --> 00:03:41,554
Ms. Keum.

36
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:45,475
Ms. Keum?

37
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:51,564
Wake up, Ms. Keum.

38
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:56,778
Ms. Lee Gae-ttong.

39
00:03:59,405 --> 00:04:00,240
What the…

40
00:04:01,157 --> 00:04:03,076
She passed out just like that?

41
00:04:27,558 --> 00:04:29,811
EPISODE 8

42
00:04:40,863 --> 00:04:42,615
Can you explain what happened?

43
00:04:42,699 --> 00:04:46,369
We had a few beers to build team spirit
in the Foreign Affairs Division.

44
00:04:46,452 --> 00:04:50,081
Ms. Keum got drunk,
so I tried to take her home by rickshaw,

45
00:04:50,164 --> 00:04:52,500
but one of the wheels fell off on the way.

46
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:53,710
That's what happened.

47
00:04:55,378 --> 00:04:56,504
It's true.

48
00:05:00,508 --> 00:05:02,760
But wouldn't you normally
carry her on your back?

49
00:05:05,638 --> 00:05:07,056
All right, I'll take over.

50
00:05:11,602 --> 00:05:12,895
Where should I go?

51
00:05:39,255 --> 00:05:40,840
Sato Hideo is in the bathroom.

52
00:05:42,592 --> 00:05:44,427
I knew she'd do something stupid.

53
00:05:48,097 --> 00:05:51,100
Just wait until you wake up
tomorrow morning.

54
00:05:57,065 --> 00:06:00,401
She might've told Sato Hideo
things she shouldn't have while drunk.

55
00:06:01,319 --> 00:06:05,573
Maybe he got suspicious and brought her
all the way here to keep an eye on us.

56
00:06:30,431 --> 00:06:33,476
<i>Whether Sato Hideo</i>
<i>has caught on to anything or not,</i>

57
00:06:34,602 --> 00:06:35,937
we should find out.

58
00:06:45,863 --> 00:06:48,574
Thank you so much for today, Mr. Hideo.

59
00:06:48,658 --> 00:06:49,826
I should get going now.

60
00:06:49,909 --> 00:06:51,869
How about a drink, just the two of us?

61
00:06:53,079 --> 00:06:54,080
No, thank you.

62
00:06:54,163 --> 00:06:56,541
It's late,
and I've already had a few beers.

63
00:06:56,624 --> 00:06:57,458
Well then.

64
00:07:00,169 --> 00:07:02,588
I didn't know
you couldn't handle a few beers.

65
00:07:02,672 --> 00:07:03,506
Well, I suppose

66
00:07:03,589 --> 00:07:06,384
your arms are probably quite sore
from carrying Seo-hee.

67
00:07:30,575 --> 00:07:32,160
Is hard liquor not your thing?

68
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:35,705
I'll get some beer.

69
00:07:35,788 --> 00:07:36,831
No, thanks.

70
00:07:43,379 --> 00:07:44,672
Mr. Hideo, you know what?

71
00:07:47,425 --> 00:07:49,760
I've always wanted
to get to know you better.

72
00:07:49,844 --> 00:07:50,970
Let's do that tonight.

73
00:08:16,579 --> 00:08:19,999
If Seo-hee made a mistake
while she was drunk earlier,

74
00:08:20,082 --> 00:08:21,626
I hope you'll forgive her.

75
00:08:22,919 --> 00:08:23,878
For my sake.

76
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:27,673
She does make up
some silly stories sometimes,

77
00:08:27,757 --> 00:08:29,300
but she means no harm.

78
00:08:29,967 --> 00:08:32,303
She loves to let her imagination run wild.

79
00:08:34,222 --> 00:08:35,306
We're out of alcohol.

80
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:38,059
Does your wife…

81
00:08:42,230 --> 00:08:43,439
know about it?

82
00:08:45,566 --> 00:08:48,027
Well, I'm sure she's caught on.

83
00:08:48,819 --> 00:08:51,614
Feelings have a way of showing,
no matter how hard you hide them.

84
00:08:56,202 --> 00:08:57,662
I'll go bring some beer.

85
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,166
Mr. Yoo, are you
ever sincere about anything?

86
00:09:07,338 --> 00:09:10,466
I've never been insincere
for a single moment.

87
00:09:14,679 --> 00:09:15,555
It's just that…

88
00:09:18,224 --> 00:09:21,227
pretending we don't know
each other's feelings

89
00:09:22,853 --> 00:09:24,480
can sometimes be for the best.

90
00:09:28,109 --> 00:09:28,985
So you mean

91
00:09:29,944 --> 00:09:31,571
your wife knows about it…

92
00:09:33,197 --> 00:09:35,324
yet you're both pretending not to know?

93
00:09:38,869 --> 00:09:40,663
I don't understand

94
00:09:40,746 --> 00:09:42,123
you people.

95
00:09:42,206 --> 00:09:44,208
Mr. Hideo, in life, there are times

96
00:09:45,209 --> 00:09:48,713
when things are too complicated
to explain to others.

97
00:09:51,966 --> 00:09:55,261
After all, I know a little more
about life than you.

98
00:09:59,348 --> 00:10:00,308
Son of a bitch.

99
00:10:03,144 --> 00:10:04,186
Pardon me?

100
00:10:22,455 --> 00:10:23,331
<i>It's just that…</i>

101
00:10:24,665 --> 00:10:27,543
<i>pretending we don't know</i>
<i>each other's feelings</i>

102
00:10:28,544 --> 00:10:30,212
<i>can sometimes be for the best.</i>

103
00:10:42,141 --> 00:10:43,184
Let go!

104
00:10:47,271 --> 00:10:49,190
Did you say complicated?

105
00:10:49,273 --> 00:10:50,691
You're just a lunatic.

106
00:10:51,275 --> 00:10:54,654
I don't know who's the lunatic here.

107
00:10:58,032 --> 00:10:58,991
What are you doing?

108
00:11:00,284 --> 00:11:01,243
Move aside, So-ran.

109
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:02,328
Stop it.

110
00:11:07,458 --> 00:11:09,835
It's not like I expect you to be a saint.

111
00:11:10,961 --> 00:11:12,338
Any decent human being would--

112
00:11:14,590 --> 00:11:16,133
Stop it already.

113
00:11:16,842 --> 00:11:18,260
Out of the way, So-ran.

114
00:11:18,344 --> 00:11:19,178
You little…

115
00:11:29,772 --> 00:11:31,148
Let this go.

116
00:11:31,232 --> 00:11:32,775
Let go.

117
00:11:36,696 --> 00:11:42,159
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

118
00:11:42,243 --> 00:11:43,077
<i>What?</i>

119
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:46,122
Wait, they got into a fistfight?

120
00:11:48,874 --> 00:11:49,834
Why on earth?

121
00:11:49,917 --> 00:11:51,836
They're both grown men.

122
00:11:54,547 --> 00:11:57,383
Why did I sleep here
instead of in my room?

123
00:11:57,466 --> 00:11:59,552
Sato Hideo slept in your room.

124
00:11:59,635 --> 00:12:01,303
He had too much to drink last night.

125
00:12:02,555 --> 00:12:03,389
I see.

126
00:12:04,515 --> 00:12:05,391
What?

127
00:12:05,474 --> 00:12:07,768
He slept in my room?

128
00:12:07,852 --> 00:12:08,894
Yes.

129
00:12:13,816 --> 00:12:14,692
Mr. Sato!

130
00:12:17,528 --> 00:12:18,529
I'm sorry!

131
00:12:19,196 --> 00:12:21,282
What do you want?

132
00:12:22,241 --> 00:12:23,617
Well, it's just that…

133
00:12:23,701 --> 00:12:26,162
I came to get something.

134
00:12:26,746 --> 00:12:29,665
Can you pick that up first?
Right by your feet.

135
00:12:31,208 --> 00:12:33,127
- What?
- It's right by your feet.

136
00:12:33,210 --> 00:12:35,129
- On your left.
- Okay.

137
00:12:38,048 --> 00:12:39,008
Here.

138
00:12:45,181 --> 00:12:46,807
Are you going to keep standing there?

139
00:12:46,891 --> 00:12:47,892
What?

140
00:12:48,517 --> 00:12:49,935
You said you came to get something.

141
00:12:51,771 --> 00:12:52,730
Right.

142
00:13:01,322 --> 00:13:02,323
And this.

143
00:13:02,406 --> 00:13:03,240
What?

144
00:13:09,288 --> 00:13:11,123
It looks like it got wet.

145
00:13:11,207 --> 00:13:13,793
Well, you see…

146
00:13:17,213 --> 00:13:18,297
This pawn ticket…

147
00:13:23,219 --> 00:13:24,470
belongs to my mother.

148
00:13:27,389 --> 00:13:29,016
I'm looking for my mother,

149
00:13:31,435 --> 00:13:33,354
but I don't even know her name yet.

150
00:13:35,523 --> 00:13:36,649
<i>"Sin Tae-jin."</i>

151
00:13:37,775 --> 00:13:39,235
If I find this man,

152
00:13:39,985 --> 00:13:41,737
I might be able to find my mother.

153
00:13:52,289 --> 00:13:53,999
Keep it for the time being.

154
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:55,835
What?

155
00:13:56,710 --> 00:13:59,380
You said you'd give it back
once I found the Jongno Pawnshop.

156
00:14:01,382 --> 00:14:02,925
I will trust you.

157
00:14:07,388 --> 00:14:08,305
I'll consider you…

158
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:12,393
my personal vault.

159
00:14:20,359 --> 00:14:23,279
Unless you're going to sew this button on,
please step out.

160
00:14:24,822 --> 00:14:26,240
Sure, I'm leaving now.

161
00:14:26,323 --> 00:14:27,533
Aren't you going to take it?

162
00:14:29,410 --> 00:14:31,495
You said you trust me.

163
00:14:41,005 --> 00:14:42,756
I'm completely out of it.

164
00:14:44,508 --> 00:14:46,886
They say when alcohol goes in,
wisdom comes out.

165
00:15:33,307 --> 00:15:34,266
Thank you.

166
00:16:00,125 --> 00:16:01,460
Excuse me, Mr. Sato.

167
00:16:04,463 --> 00:16:05,798
Interpreter Sato!

168
00:16:13,389 --> 00:16:18,519
Did I do something wrong last night?

169
00:16:20,229 --> 00:16:21,438
You see,

170
00:16:21,522 --> 00:16:24,984
I don't remember anything at all
from last night.

171
00:16:25,776 --> 00:16:30,030
I hope you'll forgive me
if I did anything stupid.

172
00:16:37,162 --> 00:16:39,748
That's because you haven't sobered up yet.

173
00:16:39,832 --> 00:16:42,668
There's no way you can't remember.
You're too smart to forget.

174
00:16:45,838 --> 00:16:47,131
What's with the "hmm"?

175
00:16:48,048 --> 00:16:50,509
It's just a little strange.

176
00:16:50,592 --> 00:16:54,054
It's not like you
to compliment me out of nowhere.

177
00:16:55,014 --> 00:16:57,891
What, have I done nothing
but give you a hard time?

178
00:16:57,975 --> 00:17:00,519
You seem a little flustered right now.

179
00:17:03,272 --> 00:17:06,275
Now I'm curious, since you're acting
like this all of a sudden.

180
00:17:06,358 --> 00:17:08,736
What on earth did I do last night?

181
00:17:08,819 --> 00:17:11,864
Why do you want me to remember so badly?

182
00:17:13,157 --> 00:17:15,868
Don't regret it
when you remember everything.

183
00:17:16,452 --> 00:17:18,120
Then let's make a deal.

184
00:17:18,203 --> 00:17:20,748
If I remember,
why don't you grant me a wish?

185
00:17:20,831 --> 00:17:22,124
Out of the blue? A wish?

186
00:17:22,207 --> 00:17:23,709
Yes, let me make a wish.

187
00:17:23,792 --> 00:17:25,878
No, I don't understand.

188
00:17:25,961 --> 00:17:28,464
You're the one who can't remember.
Why do I owe you a wish?

189
00:17:28,547 --> 00:17:31,717
I need a little incentive
to jog my memory, wouldn't you say?

190
00:17:32,468 --> 00:17:33,677
But…

191
00:17:33,761 --> 00:17:35,012
Never mind, then.

192
00:17:35,596 --> 00:17:36,889
Fine.

193
00:17:36,972 --> 00:17:38,640
I'll grant you one wish, then.

194
00:17:40,225 --> 00:17:42,061
But only one wish. That's it.

195
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:45,064
It's a deal. You can't take it back.

196
00:17:46,065 --> 00:17:46,899
Geez.

197
00:17:51,236 --> 00:17:53,072
What on earth did I say last night?

198
00:17:53,864 --> 00:17:56,492
I don't think I said I was a spy,
so that's a relief.

199
00:17:57,242 --> 00:17:58,368
Then what was it?

200
00:18:02,206 --> 00:18:03,457
Wait for me!

201
00:18:03,540 --> 00:18:05,667
Mr. Sato, wait up!

202
00:18:06,710 --> 00:18:07,753
Let's go together.

203
00:18:10,756 --> 00:18:14,134
I think Sato Hideo
has feelings for Seo-hee.

204
00:18:14,885 --> 00:18:16,220
There's no doubt about it.

205
00:18:17,137 --> 00:18:19,223
So I won't charge him
for my medical bills.

206
00:18:20,015 --> 00:18:22,601
That's not a bad deal
to make up for busting my lip.

207
00:18:23,894 --> 00:18:24,812
What?

208
00:18:47,417 --> 00:18:49,211
Please wait a moment.

209
00:18:57,177 --> 00:18:59,847
You probably don't remember anything
from last night.

210
00:18:59,930 --> 00:19:02,683
You said you wanted to make lots of money
to go to America.

211
00:19:04,518 --> 00:19:07,938
I sure said a lot of things.

212
00:19:08,021 --> 00:19:11,567
Practicing by yourself is nothing
compared to using a language in real life.

213
00:19:12,192 --> 00:19:13,986
Today is a fairly relaxed setting,

214
00:19:14,069 --> 00:19:16,864
so imagine you're in America and practice.

215
00:19:56,570 --> 00:19:59,865
What's "pneumatic tube system"?

216
00:19:59,948 --> 00:20:02,826
You can say "<i>kisokan</i>."
The Joseon term is "<i>gisonggwan</i>."

217
00:20:05,537 --> 00:20:09,124
The pneumatic tube system
is about to be officially unveiled.

218
00:20:09,208 --> 00:20:13,212
So we're starting
pre-operation inspections

219
00:20:13,295 --> 00:20:16,673
between Keijo Post Office
and the Government-General.

220
00:20:16,757 --> 00:20:17,591
How's that, darling?

221
00:20:20,969 --> 00:20:21,803
How's that, darling?

222
00:20:27,017 --> 00:20:28,101
I see.

223
00:20:28,185 --> 00:20:29,019
Okay, then.

224
00:20:29,102 --> 00:20:30,103
Good.

225
00:20:35,108 --> 00:20:36,109
Ms. Keum.

226
00:20:36,193 --> 00:20:37,027
Yes?

227
00:20:39,071 --> 00:20:40,614
The opening ceremony next week

228
00:20:40,697 --> 00:20:43,242
is an internal event
strictly for Government-General officials.

229
00:20:44,034 --> 00:20:47,246
The pneumatic tube between Keijo Post
Office and the Government-General is done.

230
00:20:47,329 --> 00:20:48,664
The pneumatic tubes between here,

231
00:20:48,747 --> 00:20:51,792
Governor-General's residence on Namsan,
and the Inspector General's residence

232
00:20:51,875 --> 00:20:53,085
are almost finished.

233
00:20:54,795 --> 00:20:55,671
I see.

234
00:20:55,754 --> 00:21:00,259
Pneumatic tubes connect buildings
over long distances with metal pipes

235
00:21:01,176 --> 00:21:04,638
and send this carrier
through the pipes at high speed

236
00:21:05,722 --> 00:21:07,099
using air pressure.

237
00:21:12,271 --> 00:21:17,359
Imagine putting a dart in a hollow reed
and shooting it out with a puff of air.

238
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:19,236
I got it.

239
00:21:19,319 --> 00:21:23,073
So you can send things
from place to place really fast.

240
00:21:23,156 --> 00:21:27,744
Yes. Pneumatic tubes are already common
in post offices in Europe and America.

241
00:21:27,828 --> 00:21:32,416
I hear some stretch as far as the distance
from Gyeongseong to Cheonan.

242
00:21:33,083 --> 00:21:34,042
Japan has them too.

243
00:21:35,168 --> 00:21:38,213
We're building the system
around the Keijo Post Office,

244
00:21:38,297 --> 00:21:40,590
connecting the main post offices,
the Government-General,

245
00:21:40,674 --> 00:21:43,051
and the Governor-General
and Inspector General's residences.

246
00:21:43,135 --> 00:21:46,680
The Government-General
plans to extend the network

247
00:21:46,763 --> 00:21:49,391
through Incheon, Kaesong,
Pyongyang, and Sinuiju,

248
00:21:49,474 --> 00:21:52,102
and eventually all the way to Manchukuo.

249
00:21:53,520 --> 00:21:54,730
Manchukuo?

250
00:22:02,696 --> 00:22:04,698
I'm so glad you've come around.

251
00:22:05,574 --> 00:22:08,076
We were so surprised
when we got the call this morning.

252
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:13,957
I suppose I took rather too long a stroll
along the road to the afterlife.

253
00:22:17,127 --> 00:22:19,588
I'm glad
you didn't settle down over there.

254
00:22:25,927 --> 00:22:28,013
I heard you sent a spy
into the Government-General.

255
00:22:29,473 --> 00:22:31,850
I hear it's the girl
I met that day briefly.

256
00:22:34,394 --> 00:22:36,063
It wasn't me!

257
00:22:36,938 --> 00:22:38,315
Move!

258
00:22:38,398 --> 00:22:39,232
Yes.

259
00:22:40,233 --> 00:22:43,028
She doesn't know you're one of us.

260
00:22:44,321 --> 00:22:45,280
I see.

261
00:22:48,867 --> 00:22:51,912
I suppose the anonymous reports
to the newspaper

262
00:22:51,995 --> 00:22:54,664
must've stopped while I was lying here.

263
00:22:54,748 --> 00:22:57,167
Just focus on getting better for now.

264
00:22:59,294 --> 00:23:00,837
All right.

265
00:23:02,839 --> 00:23:03,757
By the way…

266
00:23:05,842 --> 00:23:10,680
I heard marriage talks are underway
between Myung-nam and Sato Hideo.

267
00:23:12,099 --> 00:23:13,141
Yes.

268
00:23:13,225 --> 00:23:17,312
I'm against it. As for the reason…

269
00:23:20,607 --> 00:23:22,275
I'm sure I don't need to explain.

270
00:23:25,404 --> 00:23:26,238
Yes.

271
00:23:27,614 --> 00:23:28,740
I get it, Seog-won.

272
00:23:34,079 --> 00:23:37,374
Thank you for giving me a chance
to practice interpreting.

273
00:23:38,125 --> 00:23:40,085
I know it was sudden, but you did well.

274
00:23:40,168 --> 00:23:41,044
Really?

275
00:23:42,087 --> 00:23:44,965
I'm glad you were there.

276
00:23:45,048 --> 00:23:46,133
Had I been by myself,

277
00:23:46,216 --> 00:23:48,844
I wouldn't even have known
what a pneumatic tube system was.

278
00:23:49,428 --> 00:23:53,140
I actually thought
the P in "pneumatic" was pronounced.

279
00:23:53,223 --> 00:23:56,810
Memorizing things really isn't enough.
That really hit home today.

280
00:23:58,854 --> 00:23:59,688
Wait a minute.

281
00:24:02,899 --> 00:24:04,901
You just said "pneumatic"
with the P, right?

282
00:24:09,239 --> 00:24:11,241
I never told you the P was silent.

283
00:24:22,377 --> 00:24:25,464
How did you know
the word "pneumatic" starts with P, not N?

284
00:24:30,218 --> 00:24:33,472
Did you go through these documents
without permission?

285
00:24:37,767 --> 00:24:38,602
Yes.

286
00:24:40,187 --> 00:24:42,439
And no.

287
00:24:43,648 --> 00:24:45,692
I happened to notice them while dusting.

288
00:24:45,775 --> 00:24:48,737
There were words I didn't know,
so I memorized them.

289
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:52,324
That's how I've taught myself English
all along, by picking things up.

290
00:24:53,033 --> 00:24:54,784
- And…
- And?

291
00:24:58,246 --> 00:25:01,166
They seemed like words
that might be related to your work,

292
00:25:01,249 --> 00:25:02,584
so I thought I'd learn them.

293
00:25:02,667 --> 00:25:05,420
In case I ever got a chance to help you,

294
00:25:06,046 --> 00:25:07,923
I wanted to do well.

295
00:25:09,925 --> 00:25:11,468
Why are you working so hard?

296
00:25:12,427 --> 00:25:13,845
He has your back anyway.

297
00:25:16,348 --> 00:25:18,016
But we might break up someday.

298
00:25:21,561 --> 00:25:24,356
Then I'll be kicked out
of the Government-General.

299
00:25:25,190 --> 00:25:26,900
I'd have to find another job.

300
00:25:29,110 --> 00:25:29,986
To be honest,

301
00:25:31,988 --> 00:25:35,283
I don't want to go back
to being a live-in maid.

302
00:25:49,714 --> 00:25:52,634
Earlier today, at 11:50 a.m.,

303
00:25:53,301 --> 00:25:57,639
during the celebration of the Emperor's
birthday and Japan's victory,

304
00:25:57,722 --> 00:25:59,849
held at Shanghai's Hongkou Park,

305
00:26:00,559 --> 00:26:04,771
a <i>Josenjing </i>named Yun Bong-gil
threw a bomb at the gathering!

306
00:26:06,690 --> 00:26:09,609
They're still dealing with the aftermath.

307
00:26:10,402 --> 00:26:13,822
The commander of the forces
stationed in Shanghai was gravely wounded.

308
00:26:13,905 --> 00:26:16,324
Many people were killed or wounded.

309
00:26:17,242 --> 00:26:19,160
The culprit was arrested at the scene,

310
00:26:20,036 --> 00:26:23,081
but it's still unclear who was behind it.

311
00:26:23,665 --> 00:26:26,585
It's likely the self-proclaimed government
orchestrated it,

312
00:26:28,086 --> 00:26:29,921
but the Gugukdan might be behind it.

313
00:26:31,923 --> 00:26:33,550
The inspector general

314
00:26:34,759 --> 00:26:38,221
hasn't managed to wipe out
the Gugukdan yet.

315
00:26:38,305 --> 00:26:41,349
So that's certainly possible.

316
00:26:47,939 --> 00:26:49,774
Please forgive us, Your Excellency!

317
00:26:50,692 --> 00:26:52,152
Please forgive us!

318
00:26:58,617 --> 00:26:59,576
Inspector General.

319
00:27:00,619 --> 00:27:01,453
Yes, sir.

320
00:27:02,245 --> 00:27:04,456
Postpone the pneumatic tube
opening ceremony.

321
00:27:09,836 --> 00:27:10,795
Sir.

322
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:13,715
We've already invited the foreign press.

323
00:27:13,798 --> 00:27:16,468
How will you take responsibility

324
00:27:16,551 --> 00:27:20,388
if security is compromised
in front of the foreign correspondents?

325
00:27:21,222 --> 00:27:22,307
You!

326
00:27:22,932 --> 00:27:26,269
We suffered terrible humiliation
when the Kyeam-ri riot

327
00:27:26,353 --> 00:27:27,937
reached the foreign press in 1919.

328
00:27:28,021 --> 00:27:30,273
Have you learned nothing from that?

329
00:27:30,982 --> 00:27:31,816
Your Excellency.

330
00:27:32,692 --> 00:27:36,738
News that Yun Bong-gil,
the Shanghai bomber, is a Joseon man

331
00:27:36,821 --> 00:27:39,115
will make headlines around the world.

332
00:27:39,991 --> 00:27:41,201
That said,

333
00:27:41,284 --> 00:27:44,621
if word gets out that we're putting off
the opening ceremony,

334
00:27:44,704 --> 00:27:46,539
it'll only make things worse for us.

335
00:27:50,960 --> 00:27:54,506
Now's the time
to turn this situation to our advantage.

336
00:27:56,966 --> 00:27:59,844
We should hold the opening ceremony
as planned on a grand scale

337
00:27:59,928 --> 00:28:01,971
to show
the Government-General remains strong.

338
00:28:02,055 --> 00:28:03,598
Using event security as justification,

339
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:07,769
we'll round up the anti-Japanese activists
on our watch list.

340
00:28:13,608 --> 00:28:15,276
<i>A celebratory banquet for the opening?</i>

341
00:28:16,444 --> 00:28:20,156
They're inviting foreign journalists
and their spouses to the banquet?

342
00:28:20,240 --> 00:28:23,243
Yes. There'll be senior officials
from the Government-General

343
00:28:23,326 --> 00:28:24,703
and pro-Japanese Joseon people,

344
00:28:24,786 --> 00:28:26,871
like the president
of Joil Textile Factory, that day.

345
00:28:31,084 --> 00:28:34,504
The pneumatic tube opening ceremony
was supposed to be a small event,

346
00:28:34,587 --> 00:28:35,964
with only me and a few reporters.

347
00:28:36,047 --> 00:28:37,507
After today's bombing in Shanghai,

348
00:28:37,590 --> 00:28:40,468
it seems they intend to scale up the event
and host a banquet

349
00:28:40,552 --> 00:28:42,679
to present Joseon to the world
as a peaceful nation.

350
00:28:44,055 --> 00:28:47,016
We can't afford to miss this opportunity.

351
00:28:48,643 --> 00:28:49,477
Right.

352
00:28:58,069 --> 00:29:00,488
CHIEF OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS

353
00:29:02,657 --> 00:29:03,616
Hello.

354
00:29:08,997 --> 00:29:10,123
Oh, Sato.

355
00:29:11,708 --> 00:29:13,918
I guess the Foreign Affairs Division

356
00:29:14,002 --> 00:29:16,129
had an emergency meeting
due to the Shanghai bombing.

357
00:29:16,713 --> 00:29:17,547
Yes.

358
00:29:17,630 --> 00:29:18,882
You must have your hands full.

359
00:29:18,965 --> 00:29:21,718
You should be careful, though.

360
00:29:21,801 --> 00:29:25,096
You're the inspector general's
precious son,

361
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:26,973
yet you go around with a Joseon woman.

362
00:29:27,056 --> 00:29:28,391
Chief of Police.

363
00:29:29,517 --> 00:29:31,019
There's no need to be upset.

364
00:29:31,102 --> 00:29:33,229
Even if she's just a worthless <i>Josenjing</i>,

365
00:29:33,313 --> 00:29:35,148
it doesn't hurt
to err on the side of caution.

366
00:29:35,774 --> 00:29:39,194
All the more so in a place like
the Government-General, with so many eyes…

367
00:29:41,738 --> 00:29:42,572
on you.

368
00:29:46,618 --> 00:29:49,662
So you're wary
of some worthless Joseon woman.

369
00:29:50,872 --> 00:29:52,957
You're the chief of police
of the Government-General.

370
00:29:53,041 --> 00:29:56,252
I guess today's news from Shanghai
has made you quite nervous.

371
00:30:08,890 --> 00:30:12,602
The opening ceremony will take place
in the tube room on the first floor,

372
00:30:12,685 --> 00:30:14,979
and the banquet will follow here.

373
00:30:19,692 --> 00:30:21,653
- Can I try this?
- Sure, go ahead.

374
00:30:21,736 --> 00:30:22,821
I'll take four.

375
00:30:22,904 --> 00:30:24,197
All right.

376
00:30:24,280 --> 00:30:25,573
Thank you.

377
00:30:25,657 --> 00:30:28,493
Business is slow,
so I really appreciate you buying four.

378
00:30:30,620 --> 00:30:31,913
- Thank you.
- Sure.

379
00:30:52,058 --> 00:30:53,184
KIM SEUNG-EOK, KIM MYUNG-NAM

380
00:30:53,268 --> 00:30:54,102
How do I look, Dad?

381
00:30:54,185 --> 00:30:56,104
My dear. You look absolutely lovely.

382
00:30:56,187 --> 00:30:58,773
Sweetie, you're the prettiest girl
in the world.

383
00:30:58,857 --> 00:31:00,692
The prettiest of them all.

384
00:31:09,117 --> 00:31:12,745
After reading the Prime Minister's
congratulatory message and taking photos,

385
00:31:12,829 --> 00:31:15,498
the Governor-General will proceed
to the second-floor banquet hall.

386
00:31:15,582 --> 00:31:18,209
Make sure he doesn't cross paths
with the guests.

387
00:31:19,002 --> 00:31:23,047
The foreign press may ask technical
questions about the pneumatic tubes.

388
00:31:23,131 --> 00:31:24,048
Be fully prepared.

389
00:31:24,799 --> 00:31:26,384
Yes, understood.

390
00:31:26,467 --> 00:31:28,553
Comb every inch of Jongno!

391
00:31:29,470 --> 00:31:32,432
Find any trace of those bastards,
by any means necessary!

392
00:31:33,224 --> 00:31:34,225
- Yes, sir!
- Yes, sir!

393
00:31:37,020 --> 00:31:38,021
Get out of the way!

394
00:31:41,524 --> 00:31:42,442
Get him!

395
00:31:47,739 --> 00:31:48,740
Don't move!

396
00:31:50,283 --> 00:31:52,076
Go inside.

397
00:31:52,160 --> 00:31:52,994
Okay.

398
00:32:02,795 --> 00:32:04,380
PNEUMATIC TUBE OPENING CEREMONY PROGRAM

399
00:32:09,052 --> 00:32:11,429
<i>You'll set off a bomb at the banquet?</i>

400
00:32:12,388 --> 00:32:14,349
No. You'll never get a bomb inside.

401
00:32:14,432 --> 00:32:16,851
They'll search every single person
thoroughly that day.

402
00:32:16,935 --> 00:32:21,105
Sato Hideo's fiancée
will bring the bomb in before the banquet.

403
00:32:22,815 --> 00:32:23,900
His fiancée?

404
00:32:23,983 --> 00:32:25,276
Yes.

405
00:32:25,360 --> 00:32:29,447
He'll marry the daughter of the Joil
Textile Factory president, Kim Seung-eok.

406
00:32:32,951 --> 00:32:34,077
Young lady?

407
00:32:34,160 --> 00:32:36,204
She looks so gorgeous.

408
00:32:37,163 --> 00:32:39,666
Do you mean the beautiful lady?

409
00:32:39,749 --> 00:32:42,543
She's Kim Myung-nam,
a member of the Gugukdan.

410
00:32:44,712 --> 00:32:48,925
She recently met the inspector general
at the Government-General.

411
00:32:49,008 --> 00:32:50,093
Apparently, that day,

412
00:32:50,176 --> 00:32:53,888
she was exempted
from the body search and bag inspection,

413
00:32:53,972 --> 00:32:55,723
thanks to her status
as Sato Hideo's fiancée

414
00:32:55,807 --> 00:32:58,059
and the inspector general's
future daughter-in-law.

415
00:33:00,436 --> 00:33:02,397
A few days ahead of the banquet,

416
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:05,483
she'll go to the Government-General
with herbal medicine for her fiancé.

417
00:33:10,613 --> 00:33:14,242
There will be 30 packets
of herbal medicine in her bundle.

418
00:33:15,159 --> 00:33:18,246
The ones with the shorter string
are the bombs.

419
00:33:18,955 --> 00:33:21,416
Once she passes the security check,

420
00:33:21,499 --> 00:33:24,585
can you take the bundle,
pull out the bomb packets,

421
00:33:24,669 --> 00:33:26,129
and hide them somewhere?

422
00:33:29,757 --> 00:33:30,633
Yes.

423
00:34:02,707 --> 00:34:03,583
Hanako.

424
00:34:04,500 --> 00:34:05,460
The other day--

425
00:34:05,543 --> 00:34:07,754
Are you sorry for yelling at me?

426
00:34:10,381 --> 00:34:11,215
Yeah.

427
00:34:12,925 --> 00:34:14,177
I'm sorry, Hanako.

428
00:34:15,344 --> 00:34:16,721
That's okay.

429
00:34:26,689 --> 00:34:29,358
Hideo, I'll leave
your change of clothes here.

430
00:34:39,619 --> 00:34:43,790
Your father told me to make preparations
for your wedding in the summer.

431
00:34:50,338 --> 00:34:51,589
From now on…

432
00:34:54,092 --> 00:34:55,301
you don't have to bring those.

433
00:34:56,094 --> 00:34:56,928
Why?

434
00:34:58,596 --> 00:35:00,264
I don't want to wear them.

435
00:35:33,381 --> 00:35:35,716
Hello? This is Interpreter Sato's office.

436
00:35:42,682 --> 00:35:43,516
Over here.

437
00:35:43,599 --> 00:35:44,475
Yes.

438
00:35:48,604 --> 00:35:49,689
Hello, Ms. Kim.

439
00:35:50,857 --> 00:35:53,734
This is the daughter
of Joil Textile Factory's president

440
00:35:53,818 --> 00:35:56,195
and Interpreter Sato's fiancée.

441
00:35:57,363 --> 00:36:00,366
He recognized me
from when I came here last time

442
00:36:00,449 --> 00:36:03,578
to visit the inspector general
with my father.

443
00:36:04,871 --> 00:36:06,497
Oh my, really?

444
00:36:07,707 --> 00:36:08,541
Well…

445
00:36:09,417 --> 00:36:10,251
Yes.

446
00:36:12,420 --> 00:36:14,964
What brings you here today?

447
00:36:16,465 --> 00:36:19,844
Hideo has been so busy lately,

448
00:36:19,927 --> 00:36:22,180
so I brought him some herbal medicine.

449
00:36:23,598 --> 00:36:25,725
He's going to be thrilled.

450
00:36:27,643 --> 00:36:31,230
I'm afraid he's at a meeting
at the moment.

451
00:36:31,314 --> 00:36:33,441
Would you like to wait in his office?

452
00:36:33,524 --> 00:36:34,442
Sure.

453
00:36:35,026 --> 00:36:36,777
All right. Let me…

454
00:36:37,570 --> 00:36:39,071
Right this way.

455
00:36:39,155 --> 00:36:40,323
Just a moment.

456
00:36:40,406 --> 00:36:42,575
We need to search you
and check your bundle.

457
00:36:45,912 --> 00:36:47,955
Then I'll do it.

458
00:36:48,039 --> 00:36:51,375
We'd have to wait for Ms. Abe,

459
00:36:51,459 --> 00:36:54,212
who searches female staff in the mornings.

460
00:36:55,296 --> 00:36:56,380
I'll take the bundle.

461
00:37:06,807 --> 00:37:07,850
Let her through.

462
00:37:15,274 --> 00:37:16,108
Here you go.

463
00:37:16,192 --> 00:37:17,235
Sorry for the trouble.

464
00:37:19,987 --> 00:37:21,280
Thank you.

465
00:37:21,364 --> 00:37:24,242
I'll be sure
to let the inspector general know

466
00:37:24,325 --> 00:37:27,036
how accommodating you've been today.

467
00:37:28,913 --> 00:37:29,914
I'd appreciate that.

468
00:37:30,498 --> 00:37:31,999
Thanks for your help.

469
00:37:32,083 --> 00:37:32,917
Thank you.

470
00:37:39,924 --> 00:37:41,634
Kotaro, not that!

471
00:37:41,717 --> 00:37:42,593
Why's there a dog?

472
00:37:42,677 --> 00:37:45,304
I don't know.
I've never seen it before either.

473
00:37:46,847 --> 00:37:48,307
Was there a sniffer dog here?

474
00:37:49,058 --> 00:37:50,518
We brought it in a little while ago.

475
00:37:50,601 --> 00:37:54,272
We're taking extra security precautions
after the Shanghai bombing.

476
00:37:54,897 --> 00:37:57,775
I wonder why the sniffer dog is acting up…

477
00:37:57,858 --> 00:38:00,444
It's the smell of the herbal medicine
that's bothering it.

478
00:38:00,528 --> 00:38:02,363
That makes sense.

479
00:38:02,446 --> 00:38:05,241
Let's go already. I'm terrified of dogs.

480
00:38:06,075 --> 00:38:07,076
Let's go.

481
00:38:14,166 --> 00:38:15,084
Get the dog!

482
00:38:15,167 --> 00:38:16,669
No, Kotaro! Stop!

483
00:38:17,712 --> 00:38:18,713
Come on!

484
00:38:30,057 --> 00:38:31,767
Kotaro, settle down!

485
00:38:32,351 --> 00:38:33,602
So sorry.

486
00:38:51,537 --> 00:38:52,663
<i>That's…</i>

487
00:38:52,747 --> 00:38:54,457
<i>a bomb detonator.</i>

488
00:38:56,375 --> 00:38:57,668
Why's it barking so much?

489
00:39:10,222 --> 00:39:11,140
Oh my goodness!

490
00:39:11,223 --> 00:39:13,351
Miss, your ring fell.

491
00:39:13,434 --> 00:39:15,061
Oh no, my ring!

492
00:39:47,468 --> 00:39:48,386
What's going on?

493
00:39:49,845 --> 00:39:50,930
Mr. Sato!

494
00:39:52,848 --> 00:39:56,560
Your fiancée brought you
some herbal medicine.

495
00:40:04,110 --> 00:40:07,321
He picked up the ring for you.

496
00:40:09,782 --> 00:40:11,617
Don't show up out of the blue again.

497
00:40:13,160 --> 00:40:14,370
Thanks, but I can't take that.

498
00:40:15,538 --> 00:40:17,248
No, I insist. Please, take it--

499
00:40:17,331 --> 00:40:19,125
No, please take it back.

500
00:40:32,012 --> 00:40:33,973
Then, I'll take just this.

501
00:40:40,729 --> 00:40:42,273
Something is off.

502
00:40:45,651 --> 00:40:48,154
The dog is barking at you, Ms. Keum.

503
00:40:52,491 --> 00:40:55,953
Maybe I put on too much ointment.

504
00:40:56,036 --> 00:40:58,205
I've got quite a few bruises.

505
00:41:05,671 --> 00:41:07,506
We'll have to search you.

506
00:41:08,090 --> 00:41:09,633
Call Ms. Abe.

507
00:41:10,509 --> 00:41:11,552
This way.

508
00:41:17,266 --> 00:41:19,894
I put on the ointment
you bought me, though.

509
00:41:34,283 --> 00:41:35,117
And now

510
00:41:35,868 --> 00:41:37,119
it's barking at me.

511
00:41:40,539 --> 00:41:42,291
The dog must be a little agitated.

512
00:41:44,418 --> 00:41:46,212
It seems so.

513
00:41:47,213 --> 00:41:49,381
Yamamoto. Get the dog out of here!

514
00:41:49,465 --> 00:41:50,299
Yes, sir!

515
00:41:54,053 --> 00:41:55,054
Thanks, carry on.

516
00:41:57,932 --> 00:41:58,766
Take care, then.

517
00:42:23,374 --> 00:42:26,043
I see your meeting finished early.

518
00:42:28,754 --> 00:42:30,089
Are you badly bruised?

519
00:42:31,048 --> 00:42:33,133
- Sorry?
- You said you used the ointment.

520
00:42:34,593 --> 00:42:36,303
Yes, but I'm fine.

521
00:42:36,387 --> 00:42:38,305
I've put on the ointment you got me.

522
00:42:38,389 --> 00:42:39,431
I'll be better soon.

523
00:42:39,515 --> 00:42:41,016
Where is it this time?

524
00:42:42,476 --> 00:42:43,435
Let me have a look.

525
00:42:44,812 --> 00:42:48,482
If you keep getting hurt,
you might end up with scars.

526
00:42:48,566 --> 00:42:49,400
Where is it?

527
00:42:49,483 --> 00:42:50,818
Is it your wrist again?

528
00:42:50,901 --> 00:42:51,735
No.

529
00:42:52,570 --> 00:42:53,404
It's…

530
00:42:56,323 --> 00:42:59,159
somewhere I can't show you.

531
00:43:07,126 --> 00:43:08,669
I'd forgotten for a moment.

532
00:43:46,832 --> 00:43:48,792
How could you be with him?

533
00:44:02,389 --> 00:44:03,307
What are you doing

534
00:44:04,350 --> 00:44:05,184
so close to me?

535
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:07,478
It's just…

536
00:44:08,187 --> 00:44:09,938
you had some dust on you.

537
00:44:16,487 --> 00:44:18,656
Why on earth
do you keep seeing a man like that?

538
00:44:19,281 --> 00:44:20,115
What?

539
00:44:20,199 --> 00:44:22,785
He's abusive toward you, isn't he?

540
00:44:24,662 --> 00:44:28,082
He's supposed to treat you
like a princess.

541
00:44:29,458 --> 00:44:31,960
Treat me like a princess?

542
00:44:33,545 --> 00:44:34,922
Where did that come from?

543
00:44:38,384 --> 00:44:40,177
I'm sorry. I'm so sorry.

544
00:44:40,928 --> 00:44:42,054
That's weird.

545
00:44:42,680 --> 00:44:44,515
Why's the ground moving?

546
00:44:45,933 --> 00:44:47,101
Stay still!

547
00:44:47,685 --> 00:44:48,519
Ms. Keum.

548
00:44:49,353 --> 00:44:51,021
Ms. Keum, try to sober up.

549
00:44:51,105 --> 00:44:53,065
- Keum Seo-hee--
- Keum Seo-hee?

550
00:44:56,735 --> 00:44:58,487
But I'm not Keum Seo-hee.

551
00:45:01,615 --> 00:45:02,658
No, I'm not.

552
00:45:02,741 --> 00:45:04,618
Yeah, of course.

553
00:45:04,702 --> 00:45:06,245
Ms. Lee Gae-ttong.

554
00:45:06,829 --> 00:45:08,330
We're almost there.

555
00:45:09,873 --> 00:45:10,708
Right.

556
00:45:15,462 --> 00:45:16,380
It's that way.

557
00:45:16,463 --> 00:45:18,465
No, not that way. This way.

558
00:45:19,007 --> 00:45:21,051
Now, here.

559
00:45:22,344 --> 00:45:23,178
Get on my back.

560
00:45:23,846 --> 00:45:24,847
What?

561
00:45:24,930 --> 00:45:26,140
I said, get on my back.

562
00:45:28,976 --> 00:45:29,852
I don't want to.

563
00:45:29,935 --> 00:45:30,769
What?

564
00:45:34,732 --> 00:45:36,400
Hold me in your arms.

565
00:45:37,693 --> 00:45:38,527
What?

566
00:45:39,278 --> 00:45:40,404
I mean,

567
00:45:40,487 --> 00:45:44,783
I want you to hold me like this too.

568
00:45:44,867 --> 00:45:47,911
Like you did on the train.

569
00:45:51,749 --> 00:45:52,583
Why?

570
00:45:53,292 --> 00:45:54,251
You don't want to?

571
00:45:58,505 --> 00:46:03,302
I'm having an affair with a married man.

572
00:46:03,385 --> 00:46:06,972
So I guess I don't deserve
to be treated like a princess.

573
00:46:11,310 --> 00:46:13,103
That's not it…

574
00:46:13,187 --> 00:46:14,021
Are you crying?

575
00:46:16,231 --> 00:46:18,192
Just this once.

576
00:46:18,984 --> 00:46:19,860
Please?

577
00:46:21,195 --> 00:46:23,489
Come on, hold me.

578
00:46:25,407 --> 00:46:27,201
Like a princess.

579
00:46:30,871 --> 00:46:31,872
Prince.

580
00:46:39,004 --> 00:46:41,799
I'm afraid I don't know
what you mean by princess.

581
00:46:42,508 --> 00:46:43,842
Now…

582
00:46:44,802 --> 00:46:45,636
you remember.

583
00:46:45,719 --> 00:46:48,430
No. I don't remember anything.

584
00:46:49,264 --> 00:46:52,142
Yes, you do.
You look like you remember perfectly.

585
00:46:52,226 --> 00:46:55,103
No, I don't remember a thing, I swear.

586
00:46:55,187 --> 00:46:58,607
You promised to grant me a wish
if I remembered.

587
00:46:58,690 --> 00:47:00,359
Why would I pass up that chance?

588
00:47:00,943 --> 00:47:02,069
I mean it.

589
00:47:07,533 --> 00:47:08,367
Fine.

590
00:47:09,326 --> 00:47:11,453
I hope you never remember.

591
00:47:12,621 --> 00:47:15,791
I'll definitely remember,
so you'd better keep your promise,

592
00:47:16,375 --> 00:47:17,793
Interpreter Sato Hideo.

593
00:47:22,214 --> 00:47:23,632
Very well.

594
00:47:25,217 --> 00:47:26,051
I'll be waiting.

595
00:47:39,022 --> 00:47:42,025
You can't leave that American
even though he mistreats you.

596
00:47:45,279 --> 00:47:47,531
Is it really to keep this job?

597
00:47:53,245 --> 00:47:58,041
My personal life is none of your business.

598
00:48:02,796 --> 00:48:06,550
By the way, I heard you're getting married
to that lady from earlier.

599
00:48:09,553 --> 00:48:10,971
You two make a great couple.

600
00:48:11,054 --> 00:48:11,930
Congratulations.

601
00:48:12,014 --> 00:48:13,056
My marriage…

602
00:48:15,809 --> 00:48:17,853
is none of your business, Ms. Keum.

603
00:48:26,612 --> 00:48:28,405
Go home if you're done for the day.

604
00:49:02,314 --> 00:49:05,943
LADIES' ROOM

605
00:49:37,724 --> 00:49:38,558
That'll do.

606
00:49:42,020 --> 00:49:45,023
There's no better place to hide a bomb
than the ladies' room,

607
00:49:45,107 --> 00:49:47,526
because hardly anyone comes in here.

608
00:49:55,409 --> 00:49:56,576
Finally.

609
00:49:57,244 --> 00:49:58,620
It all worked out.

610
00:50:06,128 --> 00:50:07,504
<i>My marriage</i>

611
00:50:07,587 --> 00:50:09,548
<i>is none of your business, Ms. Keum.</i>

612
00:50:14,511 --> 00:50:16,096
It all turned out as it should.

613
00:50:19,266 --> 00:50:21,184
It's got nothing to do with me.

614
00:50:52,799 --> 00:50:54,301
My teacher is here.

615
00:50:54,384 --> 00:50:56,178
Hi, Kkot-nim.

616
00:50:56,261 --> 00:50:57,220
Have you been well?

617
00:50:57,304 --> 00:51:00,098
Yes, but we don't have a lesson today.

618
00:51:00,766 --> 00:51:01,641
You're right.

619
00:51:03,393 --> 00:51:07,522
I just wanted to study with you,
so I thought I'd stop by.

620
00:51:08,231 --> 00:51:09,816
Kkot-nim, who's that?

621
00:51:11,234 --> 00:51:13,320
He teaches me how to read hangul.

622
00:51:13,403 --> 00:51:14,362
Teacher.

623
00:51:14,446 --> 00:51:16,364
This is Geum-ok.

624
00:51:18,408 --> 00:51:19,785
- Hello.
- Hello.

625
00:51:22,704 --> 00:51:23,747
You teach her how to read?

626
00:51:23,830 --> 00:51:25,040
You scared me.

627
00:51:30,087 --> 00:51:32,005
Could you read this letter for me?

628
00:51:32,798 --> 00:51:35,509
My little brother
went to Manchuria to find work.

629
00:51:35,592 --> 00:51:37,594
The one who used to read for me
is no longer here.

630
00:51:38,637 --> 00:51:40,388
Could you read this for me, please?

631
00:51:40,472 --> 00:51:41,640
Sure, I will.

632
00:51:49,773 --> 00:51:51,066
I did open it.

633
00:51:51,149 --> 00:51:54,152
I've tried to make sense of it,
but I can't read.

634
00:51:54,903 --> 00:51:56,363
What does it say?

635
00:51:57,114 --> 00:51:58,990
Does it say he's coming back?

636
00:51:59,074 --> 00:52:00,909
It's already been three years
since he left.

637
00:52:02,285 --> 00:52:04,454
It's about money…

638
00:52:05,497 --> 00:52:06,498
Will he send me money?

639
00:52:07,707 --> 00:52:08,959
He wants you to send him money.

640
00:52:09,793 --> 00:52:10,627
Pardon?

641
00:52:15,841 --> 00:52:17,342
I'm going to wring his neck.

642
00:52:19,553 --> 00:52:21,388
Sorry, please forgive me.

643
00:52:23,557 --> 00:52:24,391
Hey!

644
00:52:25,100 --> 00:52:26,977
You got fired from your delivery job.

645
00:52:27,519 --> 00:52:30,772
Then pull a rickshaw or something.

646
00:52:30,856 --> 00:52:34,776
Or pick the pockets
of those rich men in suits!

647
00:52:35,610 --> 00:52:37,279
Forgive me,
I'll do better from tomorrow--

648
00:52:37,362 --> 00:52:38,196
Shut up!

649
00:52:39,239 --> 00:52:40,240
Shut your mouth!

650
00:52:40,907 --> 00:52:42,492
What do you think you're doing?

651
00:52:48,290 --> 00:52:49,124
Are you okay?

652
00:52:51,418 --> 00:52:53,170
Why would you beat him?

653
00:52:54,004 --> 00:52:54,880
He's--

654
00:52:54,963 --> 00:52:58,008
Hey, shut up and get lost.

655
00:53:04,347 --> 00:53:05,807
Well, well, this young master

656
00:53:07,517 --> 00:53:10,020
is sticking his nose
into other people's business.

657
00:53:11,396 --> 00:53:15,192
Or should I call you
the son of the inspector general?

658
00:53:16,693 --> 00:53:18,737
Don't be so surprised.

659
00:53:18,820 --> 00:53:21,615
You're quite well-known
around Gyeongseong.

660
00:53:22,782 --> 00:53:24,034
Yet that idiot Lee No-ma

661
00:53:25,410 --> 00:53:26,912
doesn't even know who you are.

662
00:53:28,079 --> 00:53:32,542
He'd lose his damn mind
whenever the Japanese are involved.

663
00:53:33,335 --> 00:53:36,546
Funny how he even stands guard
while you do your charity work.

664
00:53:41,593 --> 00:53:43,511
Watch yourself, young master.

665
00:53:43,595 --> 00:53:47,766
Plenty of idiots around here
got crippled fighting for independence.

666
00:53:49,059 --> 00:53:52,562
You don't want to draw their attention
by poking around here.

667
00:53:53,480 --> 00:53:54,314
What?

668
00:53:55,523 --> 00:53:56,900
Do you think this is bullshit?

669
00:53:59,152 --> 00:54:01,446
Some people around here are from Kyeam-ri.

670
00:54:02,489 --> 00:54:04,741
What would happen if they saw you?

671
00:54:04,824 --> 00:54:07,994
Wouldn't they want to burn you alive
just the same?

672
00:54:10,872 --> 00:54:12,749
What do you mean?

673
00:54:14,709 --> 00:54:16,294
Burn me alive just the same?

674
00:54:16,378 --> 00:54:18,171
Kyeam-ri, you know.

675
00:54:20,674 --> 00:54:21,967
Don't you know what happened?

676
00:54:26,596 --> 00:54:28,014
Ms. Keum.

677
00:54:28,098 --> 00:54:30,100
- Are you going home?
- Well, yes.

678
00:54:30,183 --> 00:54:33,561
Come with me. There's an important update
regarding the banquet.

679
00:54:33,645 --> 00:54:37,857
CAMELLIA CLUB

680
00:54:44,155 --> 00:54:45,282
<i>I like you.</i>

681
00:54:48,827 --> 00:54:49,786
I like you,

682
00:54:50,787 --> 00:54:51,955
Ms. Keum.

683
00:54:59,087 --> 00:55:01,339
What was the important update?

684
00:55:05,719 --> 00:55:08,847
Is the rumor true
that Interpreter Sato is getting married?

685
00:55:09,431 --> 00:55:11,099
- What?
- Word's gotten around.

686
00:55:11,182 --> 00:55:13,268
I heard his fiancée came to visit
this afternoon.

687
00:55:13,893 --> 00:55:15,770
Is he really getting married?

688
00:55:19,149 --> 00:55:19,983
Yes.

689
00:55:22,485 --> 00:55:27,157
A man like him deserves
so much better than a Joseon woman!

690
00:55:29,451 --> 00:55:32,537
If that's all, I'll get going…

691
00:55:34,289 --> 00:55:35,290
Today has been

692
00:55:36,333 --> 00:55:38,626
absolutely the worst day ever.

693
00:55:39,878 --> 00:55:40,962
Earlier today,

694
00:55:41,046 --> 00:55:45,258
a mouse fell out
of the ladies' room's ceiling vent

695
00:55:45,342 --> 00:55:48,470
and landed right on my shoulder.

696
00:55:48,553 --> 00:55:50,805
I screamed
and nearly jumped out of my skin.

697
00:55:51,306 --> 00:55:53,350
I don't want to talk about it anymore.

698
00:55:53,433 --> 00:55:57,145
Once they set a trap tomorrow,
they'll catch the mouse eventually.

699
00:55:57,228 --> 00:56:00,565
But Mr. Sato's marriage…

700
00:56:03,526 --> 00:56:05,904
Today's been so awful for me, right?

701
00:56:06,780 --> 00:56:07,614
Don't you think?

702
00:56:09,574 --> 00:56:10,408
Yes.

703
00:56:11,493 --> 00:56:12,994
I'd say so.

704
00:56:19,542 --> 00:56:20,710
Hold on.

705
00:56:21,669 --> 00:56:23,380
A mouse trap?

706
00:56:23,463 --> 00:56:24,547
Yeah.

707
00:56:24,631 --> 00:56:28,510
They'll have it set up
first thing in the morning

708
00:56:28,593 --> 00:56:30,637
inside the ladies' room vent.

709
00:56:32,097 --> 00:56:34,849
In the vent tomorrow morning?

710
00:56:34,933 --> 00:56:37,268
Yeah, early, before office hours--

711
00:56:37,352 --> 00:56:39,187
I have an emergency at home!

712
00:56:39,270 --> 00:56:40,397
Wait.

713
00:56:40,480 --> 00:56:41,731
I'm sorry!

714
00:56:41,815 --> 00:56:43,358
Ms. Keum!

715
00:56:45,485 --> 00:56:47,028
What's gotten into her?

716
00:56:48,071 --> 00:56:51,241
Oh no, Hideo!

717
00:56:51,324 --> 00:56:55,120
RECORDS ROOM

718
00:56:57,497 --> 00:56:58,456
Kyeam-ri.

719
00:56:59,958 --> 00:57:01,626
GYEONGGI POLICE DEPARTMENT REPORT FILE

720
00:57:01,709 --> 00:57:02,752
Kyeam-ri.

721
00:57:11,553 --> 00:57:12,470
1919

722
00:57:18,268 --> 00:57:19,269
Kyeam-ri.

723
00:57:19,936 --> 00:57:21,271
Please.

724
00:57:21,354 --> 00:57:23,481
I left something very important inside.

725
00:57:23,565 --> 00:57:24,482
We can't allow it.

726
00:57:24,566 --> 00:57:27,527
You need a permit
to reenter the Government-General

727
00:57:27,610 --> 00:57:28,570
after office hours.

728
00:57:30,029 --> 00:57:32,532
But Ms. Yamada was allowed in
without a permit.

729
00:57:32,615 --> 00:57:34,325
I saw her when I was leaving work.

730
00:57:34,409 --> 00:57:35,493
Ms. Yamada?

731
00:57:38,538 --> 00:57:39,873
That's right.

732
00:57:39,956 --> 00:57:40,957
So?

733
00:57:41,040 --> 00:57:42,459
But you are…

734
00:57:44,335 --> 00:57:46,004
a <i>Josenjing.</i>

735
00:57:47,839 --> 00:57:50,383
Come back during office hours tomorrow.

736
00:57:52,218 --> 00:57:53,052
But…

737
00:57:53,136 --> 00:57:54,554
What's going on?

738
00:57:54,637 --> 00:57:57,849
Ms. Keum from the Foreign Affairs Division
is requesting reentry after hours.

739
00:58:00,226 --> 00:58:01,895
Reentry, you say?

740
00:58:03,188 --> 00:58:04,522
What did you leave behind?

741
00:58:04,606 --> 00:58:08,401
The foreign press translation
about the pneumatic tube opening ceremony.

742
00:58:08,485 --> 00:58:11,905
Interpreter Sato said he'd go over it
first thing tomorrow morning.

743
00:58:11,988 --> 00:58:13,615
I meant to finish it at home.

744
00:58:16,242 --> 00:58:18,870
Please. Allow me to go back in.

745
00:58:22,499 --> 00:58:23,583
Fine.

746
00:58:24,584 --> 00:58:25,418
You may come in.

747
00:58:26,461 --> 00:58:27,795
Thank you.

748
00:58:27,879 --> 00:58:28,838
On one condition.

749
00:58:32,634 --> 00:58:33,885
Go through here.

750
00:58:39,265 --> 00:58:40,225
Why?

751
00:58:41,267 --> 00:58:42,101
You can't do it?

752
00:58:44,354 --> 00:58:48,483
You want a favor,
so show some respect as a Joseon person<i>.</i>

753
00:59:00,912 --> 00:59:01,746
Come on.

754
00:59:59,929 --> 01:00:00,805
<i>Bonne soirée.</i>

755
01:00:16,988 --> 01:00:19,741
MAY 4, 1919, CHIEF OF SUWON POLICE STATION
THE KYEAM-RI RIOT

756
01:00:26,664 --> 01:00:27,999
KEUM SEOK-JUNG

757
01:00:34,088 --> 01:00:35,882
You broke her leg?

758
01:00:36,466 --> 01:00:38,885
You damn monsters!

759
01:00:57,862 --> 01:01:01,532
KILLING OF 23 JOSEON PEOPLE AT KYEAM AND
VILLAGE DESTRUCTION BY JAPANESE SOLDIERS

760
01:01:06,829 --> 01:01:08,706
JOSEON PEOPLE'S
'NON-VIOLENT REVOLUTION' REPORT

761
01:01:41,781 --> 01:01:44,992
STATEMENT OF LIEUTENANT ISHIKAWA

762
01:01:51,999 --> 01:01:53,584
What do you think you're doing?

763
01:01:54,836 --> 01:01:56,796
ISHIKAWA NOBUYUKI

764
01:02:30,163 --> 01:02:32,123
So in the end,
you chose to do something stupid.

765
01:02:44,719 --> 01:02:45,720
Father!

766
01:02:49,557 --> 01:02:52,643
Hold on, where should I hide this?

767
01:02:57,774 --> 01:02:58,775
Not here.

768
01:03:00,568 --> 01:03:01,402
Maybe here?

769
01:03:01,486 --> 01:03:02,862
That's even riskier.

770
01:03:08,701 --> 01:03:10,119
INTERPRETER SUZUKI HIROYUKI

771
01:03:14,290 --> 01:03:15,124
Father.

772
01:03:34,811 --> 01:03:35,895
Father.

773
01:03:39,857 --> 01:03:42,193
Is this man, Ishikawa, really you?

774
01:03:42,276 --> 01:03:44,237
Did you really…

775
01:03:45,863 --> 01:03:47,615
kill 23 Joseon people in Kyeam-ri?

776
01:03:52,954 --> 01:03:53,955
Did you shoot them,

777
01:03:54,872 --> 01:03:56,040
set them on fire,

778
01:03:57,625 --> 01:03:58,793
and behead them?

779
01:04:01,003 --> 01:04:01,838
Really?

780
01:04:03,548 --> 01:04:05,132
What is it you want to tell me?

781
01:04:06,342 --> 01:04:09,262
Are you here to condemn me
for killing Joseon people?

782
01:04:10,012 --> 01:04:10,972
Father.

783
01:04:14,350 --> 01:04:16,018
Do you remember

784
01:04:16,727 --> 01:04:20,147
when I shot a Joseon boy in front of you,
saying he was a pickpocket?

785
01:04:22,066 --> 01:04:24,318
But you were the real pickpocket.

786
01:04:25,653 --> 01:04:30,032
Did you ever challenge me
for killing an innocent boy back then?

787
01:04:32,368 --> 01:04:33,452
No, you never did.

788
01:04:34,537 --> 01:04:35,621
Instead, you…

789
01:04:39,166 --> 01:04:40,167
Go away.

790
01:04:40,251 --> 01:04:41,085
Get out of here!

791
01:04:50,344 --> 01:04:51,470
Will you come with me?

792
01:04:54,640 --> 01:04:57,059
I'm asking if you'll become my son.

793
01:05:21,334 --> 01:05:23,377
<i>You took the hand I reached out to you.</i>

794
01:05:24,211 --> 01:05:27,298
You chose to become Japanese.
No one forced you.

795
01:05:27,381 --> 01:05:30,051
You changed your name to save yourself

796
01:05:30,134 --> 01:05:33,012
and survived because an innocent child
died in your place.

797
01:05:35,181 --> 01:05:37,016
So why are you acting like a fool?

798
01:05:38,184 --> 01:05:41,604
Is that <i>Josenjing </i>blood still running
through your veins, Sato Hideo?

799
01:05:50,237 --> 01:05:52,323
If you can't forget where you came from,

800
01:05:53,407 --> 01:05:56,410
remember who gave you
this life of luxury all this time.

801
01:05:58,329 --> 01:05:59,705
However foolish you may be,

802
01:06:00,748 --> 01:06:02,667
you should know you owe me some gratitude.

803
01:06:34,198 --> 01:06:35,324
Mr. Sato.

804
01:06:37,410 --> 01:06:38,828
How long have you been here?

805
01:06:39,912 --> 01:06:40,746
That's…

806
01:06:41,455 --> 01:06:42,289
Get out.

807
01:06:47,336 --> 01:06:49,130
I said, get out of here, now!

808
01:06:57,346 --> 01:06:58,764
Did you find that amusing?

809
01:07:00,933 --> 01:07:03,102
I said I didn't know

810
01:07:04,270 --> 01:07:06,272
whether to side with Joseon or Japan.

811
01:07:07,732 --> 01:07:09,150
You think I deserve this?

812
01:07:13,988 --> 01:07:14,822
Get out.

813
01:07:16,824 --> 01:07:18,909
Get out of my sight right now!

814
01:07:28,502 --> 01:07:30,629
Mr. Sato, listen.

815
01:07:33,758 --> 01:07:34,884
<i>At that moment,</i>

816
01:07:36,469 --> 01:07:38,262
<i>I remembered everything.</i>

817
01:07:39,013 --> 01:07:41,891
I remember now.

818
01:07:46,896 --> 01:07:48,606
I remember

819
01:07:50,399 --> 01:07:52,276
what I said to you.

820
01:07:54,278 --> 01:07:56,155
You know what?

821
01:07:56,238 --> 01:07:57,406
Yes?

822
01:07:58,032 --> 01:07:59,992
Would you let me call you

823
01:08:00,076 --> 01:08:03,120
by your Joseon name?

824
01:08:07,500 --> 01:08:10,503
Kim Tae-ung suits you

825
01:08:10,586 --> 01:08:14,340
so much better than Sato Hideo.

826
01:08:15,716 --> 01:08:17,384
May I call you that?

827
01:08:22,431 --> 01:08:23,933
I'm asking you.

828
01:08:32,066 --> 01:08:33,025
So…

829
01:08:35,194 --> 01:08:36,237
please don't cry.

830
01:08:46,705 --> 01:08:48,874
I'm asking you to grant my wish.

831
01:08:53,587 --> 01:08:54,630
Don't cry,

832
01:08:56,507 --> 01:08:58,425
Interpreter Kim Tae-ung.

833
01:09:47,057 --> 01:09:50,978
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

834
01:09:51,061 --> 01:09:52,688
<i>The day of the opening ceremony.</i>

835
01:09:52,771 --> 01:09:54,773
<i>We'll retrieve</i>
<i>the three bombs we've smuggled in</i>

836
01:09:54,857 --> 01:09:56,734
<i>and make time bombs.</i>

837
01:09:56,817 --> 01:10:00,529
His name was Keum Seok-jung, wasn't it?

838
01:10:00,613 --> 01:10:02,740
<i>This one's a time bomb.</i>
<i>You can make it out alive.</i>

839
01:10:03,699 --> 01:10:05,618
You'll be leaving tomorrow night.

840
01:10:06,869 --> 01:10:08,370
<i>Say that again.</i>

841
01:10:08,454 --> 01:10:09,705
What did you say?

842
01:10:10,414 --> 01:10:13,375
<i>I think it's time I gave this back to you.</i>

843
01:10:13,459 --> 01:10:15,419
<i>I know my own name perfectly well.</i>

844
01:10:15,502 --> 01:10:18,255
<i>You have your own path, and I have mine.</i>

845
01:10:18,339 --> 01:10:19,757
<i>We each have our own path.</i>

846
01:10:19,840 --> 01:10:21,217
<i>Hold on.</i>

847
01:10:21,800 --> 01:10:23,010
<i>What's this here?</i>

848
01:10:29,266 --> 01:10:31,268
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
